 2. 🤖 Synthetic everything 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A shadowy polling firm confessed this past week to faking surveys in two marquee races — then claimed the entire operation was a "short-term social experiment" on misinformation.

 Why it matters: An AI-enabled flood of synthetic and outright fabricated content is wreaking havoc on reality, poisoning the signals people rely on to understand politics, business and culture, Axios' Neal Rothschild writes. 📊 Catch up quick: Median Strategies, a previously unknown polling outfit with little public footprint, surfaced this summer with two blockbuster surveys that were quickly absorbed into the political conversation.

 In Wisconsin's Democratic gubernatorial primary, Median's fake poll showed democratic socialist Francesca Hong leading Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley by 23 points — reinforcing a broader consensus that cast Hong as the clear favorite. Crowley ultimately won by half of a percentage point. In Los Angeles, Median reported Mayor Karen Bass leading City Council member Nithya Raman by nearly 12 points. Bass cited the poll as evidence her campaign was "gaining momentum," only to delete her post after the survey was exposed as bogus. 21-year-old recent college grad Rahil Prakash revealed himself as the person behind Median's operation in an interview with The Guardian. 💥 The big picture: The cost of deception is plummeting.

 Robocalls using AI to imitate Joe Biden's voice discouraged thousands of people from voting ahead of the 2024 New Hampshire Democratic primary. Businesses are also grappling with terrifying new risks.

 North Korean operatives have built "a secret workforce" inside U.S. companies, using stolen identities, AI tools and U.S.-based facilitators to land remote jobs, according to a Wall Street Journal investigation ( gift link ). Global consultancy Arup lost more than $25 million after an employee joined a video call with deepfake versions of company executives and followed instructions to transfer money to various bank accounts. 🥶 Not even the weather has been spared: Alabama meteorologist James Spann said he fielded more than a dozen questions about an imaginary late-August "cold wave" this week after AI engagement farms churned out bogus forecasts online.

